{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Second independent reproduction, with the bound identified\n\nReproduced on 2026-10-31 during a full `make verify` for story `8257-3a28-e757-4079`, worktree `impl-8257` at `9424881459c`, `PYTEST_WORKERS=4`, macOS/arm64:\n\n```\nFAILED tests/unit/test_store_commit_maintenance_bd66.py::test_the_trace_check_detects_maintenance_inside_a_commit\n1 failed, 20134 passed, 27 skipped, 48 warnings in 7570.96s (2:06:10)\n```\n\nThe failure is a timeout, not an assertion:\n\n```\nrebar._errors.RebarError: rebar create failed (exit 1):\nError: git commit failed while holding lock: git timed out after 30s\nsrc/rebar/_lib_writes.py:166: RebarError\n```\n\nIt raises inside `_create_two_tickets_traced` at `:186`, before the test reaches any of its assertions.\n\n### The bound, and why this test is the one that hits it\n\n`src/rebar/_store/event_commit_git.py:38` sets `_GIT_TIMEOUT = 30`. That bounds every git call in the commit path.\n\nIn production that bound is comfortable, because `gitutil.py:328` defines\n\n```python\n_AUTOMAINT_OFF: tuple[str, ...] = (\"-c\", \"gc.auto=0\", \"-c\", \"maintenance.auto=false\")\n```\n\nand `event_append.py:434,:488` pass it to every `git commit`, so `git maintenance run --auto` and its repack can never run inside a commit at all.\n\nThis test deliberately removes that protection:\n\n```python\ntracker = _cross_the_maintenance_threshold(repo)\nmonkeypatch.setattr(event_commit_git, \"_AUTOMAINT_OFF\", ())\n```\n\nSo it first creates enough loose objects to *guarantee* a repack fires, then strips the suppression so the repack really does run inside the commit — all under the same 30s bound. **The test's design maximizes the cost of the operation it then wall-clock-bounds.** That makes it load-sensitive by construction, which is consistent with this ticket's title: the test added to fix a flake is itself the flake.\n\n### Isolation measurement\n\nSame tree, same host, test run alone (while 2 other `make verify` runs continued):\n\n```\n1 passed in 52.62s\n```\n\n**The test body takes 52.62s against a 30s per-git-call bound.** The margin is not large even when the test is otherwise alone, and the repack is a single call inside that 52s. Under 4-way xdist plus concurrent verifies, one git call crossing 30s is unsurprising.\n\n### Scope check — the change under test cannot be implicated\n\n`8257`'s diff is 23 files, every one under `docs/adr/`:\n\n```\n23 files changed, 958 insertions(+), 27 deletions(-)\ngit diff --name-only origin/main...HEAD | grep -v '^docs/'   ->   (no output)\n```\n\nNo source file, no test file, nothing in the store path. The change cannot affect git commit maintenance behavior, which is why I treated the failure as this ticket's rather than the story's.\n\n### Note on the predecessor\n\n`99fb-a2f3-3e9d-4116` (closed) raised the bound from 1s after the same class of failure in `test_write_survives_foreground_maintenance_repack`. The 30s figure inherited that fix. Raising it again would be the third iteration of the same move, so the durable fix is more likely to be bounding what the test asks git to do — capping the loose-object fill, or asserting on the trace without requiring the repack to complete within the commit's own timeout — than another increase."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793514159241540171,"uuid":"593c4f0e-7619-4f27-a4fb-12a86caff2f2"}